A Transformer is a type of neural network architecture, introduced in the 2017 paper "Attention Is All You Need", that is designed to work with sequences such as text. It is the foundation of almost all modern AI language models, including ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, and BERT.

The problem it solved

Before Transformers, models like RNNs and LSTMs read a sentence one word at a time, in order. This was slow, and they often forgot the beginning of long sentences by the time they reached the end.

The main idea: attention

A Transformer looks at all the words at once and uses self-attention to decide which words matter most to each other. For example, in "The animal didn't cross the street because it was tired," attention helps the model work out that "it" refers to "animal", not "street".

Main parts

Embeddings: convert words into numbers (vectors) that capture their meaning.
Positional encoding: adds word order information, since the model sees all words at once.
Self-attention: lets each word look at all the other words for context.
Feed-forward layers: further process what attention finds.
Encoder: reads and understands the input.
Decoder: generates the output one word at a time.

Why it matters

It can process words in parallel, so training is much faster on GPUs.
It handles long-range context well.
It scales well: bigger models trained on more data keep getting better.

Types of Transformers

Type	Uses	Examples
Encoder-only	Understanding text (classification, search)	BERT
Decoder-only	Generating text	GPT, Claude, Llama
Encoder-Decoder	Translation, summarization	T5, the original Transformer

